                <head>183. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>The National Security Council met on September 15, 1966, to review issues
                    concerning the pending meeting of the UN General
                    Assembly. A brief undated memorandum of the meeting by NSC staff member Nathaniel Davis records no discussion related to
                    China except a brief reference in the last paragraph, headed “Other Items,”
                    which reads as follows: “Ambassador Goldberg touched briefly on general disarmament questions,
                    Chinese representation, Korea, peace-keeping, etc. The President asked Secretary
                        McNamara if he had any comments,
                    which he didn’t, and the President closed the meeting.” (Johnson Library,
                    National Security File, NSC Meetings File, Vol.
                    4, Tab 46, 9/15/66)</p>
            </div>
